#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# sd-ai - the in-SD model helper of SD Core for Linux Solo (carry 15 of S.69 step 2, owner's decisions of 23 Oct 2026; the Core helper is gplbld/sd-ai).
#
# One short-lived process per request, started by the user's own systemd manager for each connection to the socket $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/sd-solo-ai.sock
# (sd-solo-ai.socket, Accept=true, SocketMode=0600) AS THE SOLO USER.  fd 0 is the connection: ONE JSON line in, JSON lines out.  Nothing else in
# SD connects outward; nothing else reads the keys.  Standard library only.
#
# THE ONE DIFFERENCE FROM CORE, AND WHY (owner, 23 Oct 2026, "Same-user helper"): Solo installs no OS user and no root-owned file, so the helper
# cannot be a separate OS user and cannot tell an administrator from an ordinary account by the uid.  Core's gate (the caller's uid must belong to
# the group sdaiusers) and its administrator test (sdsys or root) are therefore gone:
#   - the caller must be this helper's own user (SO_PEERCRED; any other uid is refused, and so is a connection that is not a socket: fail closed);
#   - the gate is the setting gate=yes in ai.conf, OFF as delivered, changed by `set.ai gate on|off` (an SD administrator act) or by sd-ai-ctl;
#   - the settings and the keys are changed by any process of the same user that speaks to the socket.  THAT IS THE WEAKNESS: a key is as safe as
#     the user's other private files (mode 0600, never in SD's memory, a message or the audit trail) and no safer.  The SD side stays gated: set.ai
#     and tool.ai want the administrator, and a program reaches the socket only through the kernel's $internal-only calls.
#
#   sd-ai [--state DIR] [--providers FILE]        (the unit names both; the defaults are ../ai and ai-providers beside this program)
#
# REQUEST (one line, UTF-8 JSON):  {"v":1,"op":"ask","system":"...","prompt":"...","maxtokens":1024}      or  {"v":1,"op":"status"}
#                   an ask may also carry "history": [{"q":"...","a":"..."}, ...] (at most 3; a follow-up the person chose and saw in SD's preview)
# REPLY, one line:  {"t":"ok","text":"...","in":N,"out":N,"provider":"anthropic","truncated":false,"replaced":N}
#                   {"t":"err","code":"nogate|noadmin|disabled|nomodel|nokey|nolist|toobig|limit|net|timeout|refused|busy|declined|badreply|badreq|badconf","msg":"..."}
#                   {"t":"status", ...}      (status: provider, model, key set, gate, the connection details, the limits; each provider's "model" is the one set,
#                   "default" its starting model from the providers file)
# THE SETTINGS are changed through the same socket (the caller's uid from SO_PEERCRED must be this helper's own):
#   {"v":1,"op":"set","what":"provider","value":"openai"}  {"what":"model","provider":"openai","value":"..."}  {"what":"custom","name":..,"url":..,"format":..,"auth":..,
#   "version":..,"maxparam":..}  {"what":"custom-clear"}  {"what":"ca","value":"/path"}  {"what":"limit","name":"tokens-day","value":"100000"}
#   {"what":"gate","value":"yes"}  (anything else is off)
#   {"what":"context","value":"..."} the standing text (Core S.160; "" puts the shipped default back).  status gives it as "context" with "context-from"
#   own | default | none | bad; SD sends it first with every request and does not show it in the preview.  The default is ai-context beside the providers file.
#   {"v":1,"op":"keyset","provider":"openai","key":"..."}   {"v":1,"op":"keyclear","provider":"openai"}
#   {"v":1,"op":"models","provider":"deepseek"}  -> {"t":"models","provider":..,"models":["...", ...]}: the provider's own list of model names (Core S.170)
# Every result is checked whole BEFORE it is written (one provider selected means its details are complete), the file is replaced atomically, and a key
# is never echoed or logged.  The administrator's own client is gplbld/sd-ai-ctl, which reads a key from the terminal, or from its standard input when that is
# not a terminal: since 11 Nov 26 (Core S.170) the AI page's setup dialog takes a key typed into a password field and SET.AI KEY ... HANDOVER feeds it to
# sd-ai-ctl through a hand-over file in a 0700 directory, removed whatever happens - so a key typed on the page DOES pass through SD's session on its way.
#
# WHAT IT DECIDES, IN THIS ORDER (a caller learns nothing about the settings until the gate has let them through):
#   1 THE GATE: the caller's uid from SO_PEERCRED (not from anything the caller wrote) must be this process's own, and ai.conf must say gate=yes.  Any
#     doubt (not a socket, another uid, an unreadable ai.conf) REFUSES: fail closed.
#   2 `disabled` when no provider is selected (the delivered state: provider= is empty in ai.conf); `nomodel`; `nokey`.
#   3 `toobig` (the request), `limit` (requests per hour and tokens per day, per uid, in a flock-protected file).
#   4 ONE HTTPS call to the URL of the selected provider: certificate verification mandatory (the system store, or the ca-file setting for a
#     private CA), host name checked, TLS 1.2 or later, NO insecure mode and no flag that gives one, no redirect followed, the key sent only to
#     that URL.  A reply is cut to ASCII plus newline and tab (a terminal escape sequence, an SD mark byte or a non-English letter never reaches
#     SD): typographic quotes and dashes become their plain forms, anything else becomes ?, and the number replaced is reported.
# NOTHING IS WRITTEN TO THE AUDIT TRAIL HERE: SD writes `ai request ...` BEFORE it asks, so a crash still leaves the record.  The one line this
# program writes, to stderr (the journal), has the uid, the provider, the status and the sizes - never the text, never the key.
#
# START-HISTORY:
# 11 Nov 26 dm  Carried from SD Core (owner, 11 Nov 2026: "Carry Core's dialog"): (1) Google is a shipped provider (its OpenAI-compatible endpoint) and every shipped
#               provider has a STARTING model (model= in ai-providers): the model asked for is the one set, or while there is none that one; status gives each provider's
#               "default" (Core e1061f73, 1 Nov); (2) op models, the provider's own list of model names (GET .../models with the same key and certificate rules; nothing of
#               anyone's text is sent), and a refusal carries the provider's own words (cut, ASCII, the key replaced; none for 401 / 403); https_post became https_call,
#               call_once and check_status are shared by ask and the list (Core S.170, 2cced06c, 8 Nov).  As every op here, models is the Solo user's own (Core: sdsys and
#               root).
# 11 Nov 26 dm  Carried from SD Core: (1) the standing text (Core S.160, 7 Nov, 74cf7001): set what=context keeps the text in <state>/context (mode 0600, the
#               user's own - Core's is 0640 and sdai's), status hands it, or the shipped default tools/ai-context, to the Solo user (Core: a gate member or the
#               administrator; Solo's status is the one user's already); (2) a follow-up's "history" (Core 3 Nov, d99b1f6d): at most 3 earlier exchanges
#               {"q","a"}, sent as earlier user / assistant messages before the request in both wire formats, counted in the bytes-request limit, any other
#               shape badreq.  Solo's kernel has sent the history form since its op_ai.c carry; this helper ignored it until now.
# 23 Oct 26 dm  Solo carry 15: the same-user helper (above).  Carried from Core ca89081 with the gate and the administrator test replaced; every other rule - the
#               validators, the limits, the hygiene, the https call - is Core's unchanged.
# 23 Oct 26 dm  (Core) The settings (set, keyset, keyclear) are made here, by sdsys and root, instead of by sd-elevate verbs as the plan first said: the file and the keys are
#               sdai's own, the one check is the peer's uid, and the same validators serve both the reading and the writing.
# 23 Oct 26 dm  New.  Group A of plan-ai-step2: non-streaming (the framing is line-based so a streamed reply can follow), two wire formats
#               (anthropic, openai), the three shipped providers and the owner's fourth, Custom.
# END-HISTORY

